## 5. Cleanup and rollback

### Windows

Remove the test Run-key entry and temporary registry key:

```powershell
Remove-ItemProperty -Path HKCU:\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Run -Name "CySA_Benign" -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
Remove-Item -Path HKCU:\Software\CySA -Recurse -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
```

### Ubuntu

Restore the SSH configuration backup created before the test:

```bash
sudo mv /etc/ssh/sshd_config.bak /etc/ssh/sshd_config
sudo systemctl restart ssh
```

After cleanup, verify that the restored SSH configuration is correct and that the audit rule is still present only if you intend to continue monitoring the file. Remove the test audit rule when the lab is finished if you no longer need it.

## 6. Completion and verification status

The supplied screenshots demonstrate Windows audit-policy configuration, Windows process-creation events, Sysmon process events, registry monitoring and Linux auditd activity.

Two verification points still deserve attention before describing every objective as fully complete:

1. The Windows Security Event ID 4688 screenshot does not clearly show a populated command-line field, even though Sysmon process events show command-line information. Verify the Windows 4688 details after enabling command-line auditing.
2. The Linux screenshots establish local auditd setup/activity, but the Splunk search screenshot does not conclusively prove that actual `PATH`/`SYSCALL` records for `/etc/ssh/sshd_config` reached Splunk. Verify with `auditctl -l`, `ausearch -k sshd_config_change -i` and a Splunk search that returns those actual records.

Do not mark those two items complete until the corresponding event fields and Splunk records have been checked.

